# The sources of what GCSL code means beyond its text, as gcsl::SEMANTICS
# was last set or confirmed for (see tests/semantics.rs). Each line after
# the digest says why it was written again without raising SEMANTICS.
semantics 1
digest 294653ab219a5abc5339c77577296368edb7754fd69c80b699469e280a9641f5

PEI-1775: recording of what a run used (functions, top-level cells, contexts), for tests only (cfg test or the coverage feature); what code means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: block parameters, a new form: a trailing block where a function was expected was a check error, so what every program that checked means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: a host is told of the failures a program catches, and `raise` and `try`, new forms: a name `raise` that named nothing and `try` before an expression were errors, but a call of a function named `try` with only a trailing block, which no known program has; what every other program that checked means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: a forced yield in graph mode and a host's say over a stopped unit's clocks, both off unless a host asks; what code means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: a parameter's type inferred from its default, a new form that was a syntax error; what every program that checked means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: host operations and handles, new forms that were syntax errors; what every program that checked means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: a host can ask whether plain data holds a handle, and a deferred call's inputs refuse one; no program that checked before handles existed can make one, so what every such program means is unchanged.
PEI-1786: the body of a definition whose def-type's definitions return `{}` ends with a statement where its last expression is no `{}`, which was a check error; a last expression that is `{}` is its result as before, so what every program that checked means is unchanged.
PEI-1788: in graph mode only the tasks that may have their turn are woken to look for it, and a task's path is found once while it is set aside; which effects are made, in what order, and what code means are unchanged.
PEI-1788: in graph mode the tasks waiting for their turn are kept apart in program order, and a task split off keeps only the paths of the scopes it was split off inside; which effects are made, in what order, and what every node is named are unchanged.
PEI-1789: a loop that stands as a statement collects nothing when its body ends with a call of an impure function, which was an unused-value error; every loop that checked before means what it meant.
